        kind external
        role origin
        document "outside-review-2026-11-03"
        use """
            Section 6 and the Medium documentation row: screenshots, published examples, tutorials, crate-level docs, and a contribution path independent of AKR.
            """
    }
}

record pixelkit.work.publish-packaging/1 : work {
    title "Packaging for a crates.io release, held until the owner decides the toolkit is ready"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "Cargo.toml", path "crates/*/Cargo.toml" ]
    intent """
        When and if publishing is decided: cargo publish --dry-run for each crate in the order raster, text, shell, ui, windowing, kms, liminalkit, and a v0.5.0 tag. Already in place: version 0.5.0 derived per @pixelkit.decision.versioning-method with matching requirements on every path dependency, a search-oriented description with keywords and categories on every crate, a README per crate referenced from its manifest, LICENSE files at the root, CHANGELOG.md with every generation, the test fonts out of every publishable crate per @pixelkit.decision.test-fonts-unpublished-crate, and cargo package verifying the leaf crate.
        """
    note """
        On 2026-11-03 the owner first deferred publishing, then asked that the crates be prepared for publication without being published. Done in commits 900358a and ff2315c: descriptions written so they surface in crates.io searches, keywords and categories on all seven publishable crates, cargo package --list reviewed for every crate, the fonts moved into the unpublished liminalkit-test-fonts crate (liminalkit-text now packages only its sources, README and example), and the version set to 0.5.0 from the commit history. Nothing has contacted the registry for a publish, including a dry run, which is the owner's call. The dependent crates cannot be dry-run until their path dependencies exist on crates.io, so the first real publish is sequential in the listed order.
        """
    after [ @pixelkit.work.performance-comparison, @pixelkit.work.public-docs ]
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "outside-review-2026-11-03"
        use """
            Section 4, recorded for completeness; the recommendation to publish was declined by the owner.
            """
    }
}

record pixelkit.work.raster-simd-coverage/1 : work {
    title "Measured exact SIMD coverage in raster kernels"
    state proposed
    intent """
        Keep coverage merging bit exact while adding SIMD only where profiles
        justify it. The initial u8x32 path handles rows of at least 1024 bytes;
        shorter glyph spans retain the scalar loop. Compare scalar and vector
        medians for short and long rows on aarch64 and x86_64, then expand to
        alpha coverage blending or opaque bitmap rows only after equivalent
        output and a measured speed benefit. Preserve clipped tails and portable
        behavior on unsupported targets.
        """
}

record pixelkit.work.scroll-module/1 : work {
    title "Shared scrolling: lossless wheel input, a wheel interpreter, an optional motion controller, and scroll regions"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "crates/liminalkit-shell/src/input.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-shell/src/scroll/**",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/**",
        path "crates/liminalkit-windowing/src/host.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit/src/app.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Scrolling by notched wheel, free-spin wheel, precision touchpad and dragging should feel good in the LiminalDoc PDF reader and book editor, with no input level ignored. As found, wheel input was lost between the OS and the widget (fixed 24 physical px per line, no timestamps, per-frame summing, per-frame integer rounding, horizontal discarded, the text area pulling the view back to the caret), and neither application had cadence-based acceleration or wheel smoothing. Delivered in eight stages, each shippable: lossless input and hardware trace capture; clock and replay; wheel interpreter; minimal motion controller and tuning example; LiminalDoc integration; scroll regions, routing and interactive scrollbar; drag physics and native hints; calibration.
        """
    note """
        Owner approved the staged plan on 2026-11-19 after an outside review that asked for the interpreter/motion split, earlier trace capture and LiminalDoc integration as an acceptance stage. Constants are starting points until calibrated on a notched wheel, a free-spin wheel and a precision touchpad; no touchscreen is available.
        """
    trace [
        "Owner asked for a scrolling module with no input level ignored; scouting found five places wheel input was lost between winit and the widget, so stage 1 fixes loss before adding any feel.",
        "winit on Windows hands over the raw wheel value divided by 120, so exact 120ths are recovered there only; other platforms' line deltas stay lines, as the outside review asked.",
        "ScrollEvent, on_scroll and on_scroll_event were kept rather than removed: the LiminalDoc writer reads them by path dependency and would not build until its own migration in stage 5.",
        "ScrollState keeps integer offsets plus a carried fraction per axis, because text and images draw at whole device pixels and hit-testing must match what was drawn.",
        "Movement refused at a bound is dropped, not carried: otherwise scrolling back from an end would first have to use up the excess.",
        "The text area re-revealed its caret every focused frame, undoing wheel scrolls; it now follows only when caret, text length or focus changed, which also covers IME commits made outside the widget.",
        "A wheel line is the content's own line (table row, text line) times the system lines-per-notch; content without lines uses 100/3 logical px so a notch is 100 logical px at any scale.",
        "Horizontal offsets exist in ScrollState but table, list and text area still have no content wider than their viewport; sideways scrolling of those waits for scroll regions in stage 6.",
        "Trace capture moved into stage 1 on the review's advice: the classifier in stage 3 is to be written against what the three devices actually send, not against assumptions.",
        "Stage 2: nothing in the toolkit reads the clock while drawing; the host passes frame time in so recorded input replays identically in tests.",
        "Frame requests last one frame and must be renewed, so a window returns to sleeping the moment nothing is moving.",
        "Animation pacing uses the display's refresh interval, not ControlFlow::Poll: softbuffer has no vsync, so Poll would spin a core repainting.",
        "GetMessageTime was rejected as the sample timestamp: tick resolution of 10 to 16 ms cannot resolve a free-spin wheel; delivery time plus an after_busy flag is used.",
        "Stage 3's classifier waits on traces from the owner's three devices; scroll_capture exists for that and has only been smoke-started, not used with a device.",
        "Stage 3: the owner's recordings showed both wheels send only whole notches, so the classifier reads whole against fractional and leaves the two wheels to cadence.",
        "A fraction is conclusive and flips the class at once; a whole notch is weak evidence, so three in a row are needed to leave Fine.",
        "A lone first notch is treated as a notch immediately but reported unsettled: an Unknown class would have made a wheel's first click behave unlike its second.",
        "Continuous input is never accelerated because the touchpad recording shows the system already coasting after a stroke; adding gain or a fling would double it.",
        "Units carry a line, a page and a pixel in the caller's own unit, since the LiminalDoc editor scrolls in fractional pages, not pixels.",
        "Stage 4: the motion controller is one axis with direct, chase, timed animation and interruption only; flings and springs wait until the basic feel is judged on a screen.",
        "Replay tests compare positions at identical instants across four frame rates on all three recordings; an earlier nearest-instant comparison failed on the test's own slack, not the controller.",
        "Stage 5: both LiminalDoc apps adopted the interpreter alone; neither took the motion controller, since each already owns a position model with its own rules.",
        "ScrollEvent, on_scroll and on_scroll_event were still not removed: LiminalDoc no longer uses them, but restaurant-pos, salon-desktop and the LegeOS shell could not be checked from here.",
        "The reader needed liminalkit-shell only for scroll types, confirming the module is usable without the host, Input or any widget.",
        "Stage 6: winit keeps delivering moves and the release while a button is held, so cursor_left no longer ends a drag; blur and cancel_pointer remain the ways out.",
        "Nested routing uses last frame's region list because an outer region draws, and would claim the wheel, before the list inside it exists this frame.",
        "Who a roll belongs to is latched for 400 ms: the recordings show gaps up to 136 ms inside a roll, and a page must not lurch when a list runs out.",
        "Tuning lives inside ScrollState because table, scroll_list and text_area have fixed signatures; a separate options argument would have changed every caller.",
        "With no frame clock the state settles at once, which kept all 199 existing UI tests passing unchanged and leaves low-level hosts as they were.",
        "Left-button drag-pan was left out: controls act on press, so it needs the arm-then-threshold handoff and belongs with fling in stage 7.",
        "Not done in stage 6: sideways scrolling of the table, a scrollbar on the text area, overlay or auto-hiding bars, and edge auto-scroll while selecting.",
        "Owner reported the grab hand staying after a middle click: App seeded each frame's screen cursor with last frame's control request, so nothing restored the arrow.",
        "Stage 7: fling is off by default because with a mouse a drag that ends is over; the PDF reader makes the same choice for non-touch input.",
        "Overscroll was not added to the new controller: Windows wheel and touchpad streams carry no phase to spring back on, and no touchscreen is available to judge it.",
        "The fling was written as its own closed form instead of extracting ScrollAxis's physics, which would have meant refactoring tested touch code for three lines of maths.",
        "Wheel settings and the source hint use two hand-declared user32 functions in win.rs, the crate's one unsafe module, so no dependency was added.",
        "The touchpad hint only ever adds SourceHint::Touchpad when the system volunteers it; registering the thread as touchpad-aware changes pointer delivery and was deliberately not done.",
        "Stage 8: the native recording marked none of 89 touchpad messages, so the source hint stays experimental and nothing depends on it.",
        "Presets were left as written: the report shows the finely graded wheel reaching full gain under every accelerating preset, which only the owner's hand can call right or too much.",
        "Interpreting and moving one sample costs about 50 ns in a release build, so the scroll path is not a performance concern.",
        "The old ScrollEvent, on_scroll and on_scroll_event hooks remain for the owner to remove once other applications are checked.",
        "cargo doc with warnings denied still fails in the facade crate on doc links in native.rs, which belongs to the uncommitted native-controls work and was left alone."
    ]
    implements [
        @pixelkit.decision.scroll-apps-own-navigation,
        @pixelkit.decision.scroll-distance-at-event-time
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record pixelkit.work.smooth-rounded-surfaces/1 : work {
    title "Opt-in anti-aliased rounded surfaces for widgets (Ui::set_smooth_edges)"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "crates/liminalkit-raster/src/shapes.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/chrome.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/dropdown.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/form.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/overlay.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/textarea.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/toast.rs",
        path "crates/liminalkit-ui/src/widget.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Give desktop apps smooth rounded surfaces without changing anything for existing apps. Ui gains an
        opt-in `set_smooth_edges` / `with_smooth_edges` switch (off by default); with it on, every rounded
        surface the widgets draw (buttons, panels, fields, dropdowns, menus, overlays, toasts, badges, bars,
        tabs, text buttons) goes through `Ui::rounded` / `Ui::fill_rounded`, which use the analytic
        anti-aliased path fill, via a new `Painter::rounded_rect_aa` (outer shape in the edge colour, inset
        shape in the fill colour). Off, output is byte-identical to before, so golden screens and Raspberry
        Pi redraw cost are unaffected. Requested for Lege's desktop GUI, whose button outlines were stepped.
        """
    acceptance {
        check smooth-corners {
            statement """
                With the switch on, only corner pixels change and they gain intermediate shades; straight edges on whole pixels stay on the same pixels.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p liminalkit-raster the_smooth && cargo test -p liminalkit-ui smooth_edges"
            verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.smooth-rounded-surfaces-suite/1 ]
        }
        check unchanged-by-default {
            statement """
                Without the switch, a frame is byte-identical to before (the existing suites pass unchanged, and a button drawn with the switch off equals one drawn without touching it).
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test --no-fail-fast"
            verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.smooth-rounded-surfaces-suite/1 ]
        }
    }
    author "dk"
}

record pixelkit.work.text-rendering-quality/1 : work {
    title "Gamma-corrected text coverage, opt-in vertical hinting and stem darkening"
    state completed
    intent """
        Make small UI text (11 to 17 px, both polarities, scales 1 to 2) crisper
        and more evenly weighted: correct glyph coverage for the sRGB blend with
        a per-pair coverage curve (text and background lightness), and offer
        opt-in vertical grid fitting and horizontal stem darkening through a
        defaulted TextRendering held by TextCache, so existing callers compile
        unchanged and TextRendering::UNCORRECTED reproduces the old output.
        """
    note """
        Defaults: gamma 1.8, contrast 0.5, Hinting::None, stem_darkening 0.
        Vertical hinting measured about 8% less grey at horizontal edges over
        9-34 px but only 2% at sizes whose x-height is already whole, so it is
        opt-in. Before/after sheets were rendered outside the repository.
        """
    acceptance {
        check uncorrected-identical {
            statement """
                Rendering with TextRendering::UNCORRECTED reproduces the previous toolkit's pixels byte for byte.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.text-rendering-uncorrected-identical/1 ]
        }
        check workspace-tests {
            statement """
                The workspace test suite passes, including the curve, mapped-span and rendering-option tests.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test --workspace"
            verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.text-rendering-tests/1 ]
        }
    }
    author "dk"
}

record pixelkit.work.text-shaping-fallback/1 : work {
    title "Font fallback across scripts, bidirectional text, and an optional mature shaper behind a feature"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "crates/liminalkit-text/**" ]
    intent """
        Keep the dependency-free renderer as the default path and add what a general caller needs: per-run font fallback that walks a FontSet's chains by script and coverage rather than only by missing glyph; Unicode bidirectional reordering for mixed-direction lines; and an optional `shaper-rustybuzz` (or equivalent) feature that routes shaping through a full OpenType engine while liminalkit-text keeps rasterization, caching and wrapping. The feature must be off by default and must not change output for the bundled Latin and Thai fonts when off. Measure the binary-size and startup cost of the feature so an appliance build can refuse it knowingly.
        """
    note """
        Bidi slice done 2026-11-03 (commit 900358a): the `bidi` feature of liminalkit-text, per @pixelkit.decision.bidi-behind-feature, with tests for an override, a mixed line, mirrored brackets and marks staying on their base in a reversed run, in CI with and without the feature. Direction controls are never drawn, feature or not. Still open: script-aware fallback (today fallback is per character by coverage, which is correct for Latin+Thai but splits a run of a script the primary partly covers), Arabic-style joining which needs lookup selection by script and feature tag, and the external-shaper decision with its size and startup measurement. Right-to-left editing in text fields (visual caret, selection) is a UI-crate follow-up once a right-to-left face is part of the test fixtures.
        """
    after [ @pixelkit.work.adoptability-pass ]
    implements [ @pixelkit.policy.explicit-state-toolkit ]
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "outside-review-2026-11-03"
        use """
            The High row for text: font fallback, broader Unicode shaping, bidirectional text, and the suggestion of an optional rustybuzz integration.
            """
    }
}

record pixelkit.work.touch-interactions/1 : work {
    title "Experimental original touch interaction physics and playground"
    state completed
    intent """
        Implement original renderer-independent tap/drag/pinch arbitration, release
        velocity, configurable inertial/elastic/snapping scrolling, and a touch tuning
        playground with bounded recording, deterministic replay and motion graphs.
        Use no third-party gesture or physics implementation. Clearly label the new
        APIs and example experimental and behavior/hardware/feel untested. Perform
        compile and formatting checks only; provide regression tests for later runs.
        """
    note """
        EXPERIMENTAL / UNTESTED. Implementation and compile checks are complete only.
        The twelve regression cases are provided but unrun. Interactive behavior,
        physical hardware, latency and subjective touch feel are explicitly untested.
        Recording is memory-only. Nested parent capture and fling transfer remain
        application policy; the physics API supplies unconsumed hard-bound drag deltas.
        """
    verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.touch-interactions-compile/1 ]
}

record pixelkit.work.touchscreen/1 : work {
    title "Touchscreen UI building blocks"
    state completed
    intent """
        Add host-independent touchscreen primitives, contact lifecycle and gesture
        tracking, an explicit existing-widget pointer adapter, native winit delivery,
        and a runnable touch diagnostics example. Preserve hardware-independent
        rendering and distinguish host event timing from physical touch latency.
        """
    verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.touchscreen-tests/1 ]
}

record pixelkit.work.ui-capabilities/1 : work {
    title "Dialogs, menus, overlays, drag-and-drop and multi-line text, as caller-owned state"
    state completed
    scope [ path "crates/liminalkit-ui/**" ]
    intent """
        Add the capabilities an operational application reaches for next, each in the shape Dropdown already has: a value the screen owns, an input prepass that claims clicks and keys before the background draws, and a draw call that runs last. A modal dialog (scrim, focus trapped inside, Escape and a result enum); a context menu and a menu bar sharing the popup machinery; a generic overlay/popover anchored to a rect; in-app drag-and-drop between lists with a caller-owned DragState (OS file drops already arrive through on_file_drop); a multi-line text field with wrapping, vertical scrolling and the same grapheme-boundary editing as the single-line one. Every one of these must state in its signature what it owns and what persists between frames.
        """
    note """
        Done 2026-11-03 in liminalkit-ui: overlay.rs (Overlay with open_dialog/open_popover, Placement, its own Focus, keys taken in handle_input and restored in draw via the new Input::restore_keys), menu.rs (Menu over Dropdown's popup with Ui::context_menu and menu_width), drag.rs (Drag<T> with source/target/ghost and a press threshold that leaves clicks to the control), textarea.rs (TextAreaState over TextFieldState: wrapped paragraphs, Up/Down keeping the column, line Home/End, Enter, click placement, caret kept in view). A menu bar was not built: a context menu at a point covers the operational case and a bar is the same popup anchored to a strip, left for when a screen needs it.
        """
    acceptance {
        check capability-tests {
            statement """
                Overlay, Menu, Drag and TextAreaState each have state-level tests covering open, claim-before-background, dismiss, and the keyboard paths, and the workspace suite passes.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test --workspace --features liminalkit/test-fonts"
            verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.capabilities-pass-tests/1 ]
        }
        check lint-clean {
            statement """
                The new modules add no clippy or rustdoc warnings.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --features liminalkit/test-fonts,liminalkit/accessibility && RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --no-deps --features liminalkit/test-fonts,liminalkit/accessibility"
            verified_by [ @pixelkit.evidence.workspace-lint-and-docs-clean/1 ]
        }
    }
    after [ @pixelkit.work.adoptability-pass ]
    implements [ @pixelkit.policy.explicit-state-toolkit ]
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "outside-review-2026-11-03"
        use """
            The Medium row for UI capabilities: dialogs, menus, overlays, drag-and-drop, richer text input.
            """
    }
}

record pixelkit.work.windowing-crate/1 : work {
    title "Windowing crate: host move, multi-window, panes, in-app windows"
    state completed
    intent """
        Move OS-window hosting out of pixelkit-shell into a new pixelkit-windowing
        crate, add a multi-OS-window runner, and add in-app draggable panes plus
        in-app floating windows behind small caller-owned state types, with unit
        tests for the layout and drag state machines.
        """
    note """
        User decisions (2026-09-29): both OS-level and in-app windows; new crate
        named pixelkit-windowing. Event vocabulary used by shell's Input collector
        (KeyInput, Modifiers, MouseButton) stays in pixelkit-shell; host/app
        protocol types move with the host.
        """
}
